===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|seisin}}, {{m|enm|seysen}}, from {{der|en|fro|seisin}}, from the verb {{m|fro|seisir}}, from {{der|en|la-eme|sacio|sacīre}}, from the same {{der|en|ine-pro|-}} root as {{cog|got|𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽}} and {{cog|ang|settan}}. More at {{l|en|seize}}, {{l|en|seise}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|law|historical}} An [[entitlement]] to a [[freehold]] estate with a right to immediate possession.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The act of taking possession.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The thing possessed; [[property]].
#* {{RQ:Hale Common Law|part=Analysis|section=XLIII|sectionname=Concerning Wrongs which Carry with Them an Amotion of Possession|page=131|passage=''Diſſeiſin'' is a large Title, and is an unlawful Entry and Ouſter of him that has an actual '''Seiſin''' and Freehold.}}

